The company gained significant recognition for its pioneering work in the Internet of Things (IoT) space, most notably with the creation of the ''Little Printer'' in 2012. This was a small, wireless thermal printer that delivered personalized content, such as news headlines, puzzles, and messages from social networks, creating a miniature newspaper. The Little Printer was powered by the company''s proprietary platform, BERG Cloud, which was described as a "nervous system for connected products". The platform was designed to provide the necessary cloud infrastructure and services to make it easier for hardware innovators to develop their own connected devices, handling aspects like user management, APIs, and fleet management. BERG offered developer kits, including a Devshield compatible with Arduino and Raspberry Pi, to encourage experimentation on its platform. 

In 2013, the company pivoted from a consultancy model to focus on commercializing the BERG Cloud platform and its associated products, securing funding to support this shift. However, BERG struggled to achieve a sustainable business model in the nascent connected products market. Faced with operational challenges and market pressures, the company announced its closure in September 2014, ceasing its services and partnerships. 

Keywords: connected hardware, Internet of Things, IoT platform, design consultancy, product invention, smart devices, Little Printer, cloud services, hardware innovation, prototyping platform, wireless devices, connected products, user management API, hardware development kit, Raspberry Pi projects, Arduino projects, thermal printer, personalized content delivery, digital-physical products, technology strategy', '["database","Venture Capital","Prototype","Subscription","SaaS","content management system","DT and LS","it infrastructure","Developer Tools","b2b","web servers","Hard Tech","programming languages","caching","javascript libraries","intelligence and analytics","Enterprise Software","analytics","operating systems & computing languages","web analytics","Early stage","IoT InternetOfThings","blogs","Deep Tech","operating systems","maps"]', '["startup","vc_backed","funded"]', 62.38, 6400, 0, 0, 5909089, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"year":2018,"revenue":2727272,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":38},{"year":2019,"revenue":2954544,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":40},{"year":2020,"revenue":3106059,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":41},{"year":2021,"revenue":3409090,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":45},{"year":2022,"revenue":3409090,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":45},{"year":2023,"revenue":3636362,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":52},{"year":2024,"revenue":3636362,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":46},{"year":2025,"revenue":3636362,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":47},{"year":2026,"revenue":3636362,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":5909089,"employees":46}]', '2026-11-05T15:53:18.240Z', 'GBP', '2026-11-05T15:53:18.240Z');

The company provides a comprehensive software-as-a-service (SaaS) platform with a subscription-based pricing model. Its features include appointment scheduling, online booking, client and employee management, POS systems, cash and stock management, marketing automation, and reporting tools. BEWE.io also develops personalized, white-label mobile apps for its clients to foster direct customer relationships and loyalty. Recently, the company has integrated an AI assistant named Linda to help businesses with data analysis and personalized marketing strategies. 

BEWE.io serves a diverse clientele, including hair salons, spas, gyms, yoga and Pilates studios, barbershops, and psychologists, primarily in Spain and Latin America. The company has been expanding its operations into the United States. To date, BEWE.io has raised a total of $8.53 million over five funding rounds. Key investors include DILA Capital, Seaya Ventures, The Venture City, and Mountain Partners. 

