The company''s core product is the Versius system, a modular and portable surgical robot designed to integrate into existing operating rooms without the need for significant infrastructure changes. This system biomimics the human arm and features fully wristed instruments, 3D HD vision, and an open surgeon console. These features provide surgeons with enhanced dexterity and precision, while the open console design aims to reduce surgeon fatigue and improve communication with the surgical team. The modular design allows hospitals to use only the number of robotic arms needed for a specific procedure and easily move the system between departments. Versius is used across multiple surgical specialties, including gynecology, general surgery, urology, and colorectal surgery. 

CMR Surgical''s business model involves partnering with hospitals and healthcare providers, offering flexible financing options such as cost-per-case managed services alongside capital sales. This approach is designed to lower the barrier to adoption for hospitals of various sizes, including those with budget or space constraints. The company also provides a comprehensive digital ecosystem that includes the Versius Connect app and a clinical registry to capture data and support continuous learning for surgeons. Headquartered in Cambridge, UK, with a global manufacturing hub in Ely, Cambridgeshire, CMR Surgical has expanded its presence to over 30 countries across Europe, Latin America, Asia, the Middle East, and Australia. The company achieved unicorn status in 2019 and was valued at $3 billion in 2021. It has raised significant funding, including a $600 million Series D round in 2021 and over $200 million in 2025, to fuel its global commercialization, particularly its entry into the U.S. market. 

