The business model of Cabify is straightforward. Users download the Cabify app, create an account, and can then book rides. The app shows the price of the journey upfront, ensuring transparency and eliminating any surprises. This feature is particularly appealing to users who want to manage their travel expenses more effectively. Cabify also offers a corporate service, allowing companies to manage their employees'' travel needs through a single platform. This service includes features like real-time journey tracking, cost control, and the ability to set price, time, and zone limits.

Cabify makes money by taking a commission from each ride booked through its platform. This commission is a percentage of the fare paid by the user. The company also offers subscription plans for businesses, providing them with additional features and benefits in exchange for a recurring fee.

Safety is a key selling point for Cabify. Each journey is geo-tracked, and drivers are thoroughly vetted to ensure they meet the highest quality standards. The company has also implemented extra safety and hygiene measures to protect users, especially in light of the COVID-19 pandemic. Users can share their route in real-time with friends and family, adding an extra layer of security.

In summary, Cabify is a ride-hailing service that prioritizes safety, quality, and transparency. It serves both individual consumers and businesses, operating primarily in Latin America and Spain. The company generates revenue through ride commissions and corporate subscriptions.

