The company''s core product, originally named Digger and now rebranded to OpenTaco, functions as an IaC orchestration tool that runs natively within a user''s CI environment. This architecture provides two main benefits: security, as cloud credentials and other sensitive data do not need to be shared with a third-party service, and cost-effectiveness, by utilizing existing CI compute resources instead of paying for a separate, parallel system. The tool allows DevOps and platform engineers to manage Terraform/OpenTofu workflows through pull requests, enabling ''terraform plan'' and ''terraform apply'' commands directly from PR comments. Key features include PR-level locking to prevent race conditions, drift detection to identify discrepancies between code and the actual infrastructure state, and support for Open Policy Agent (OPA) for role-based access control (RBAC). 

In June 2025, Digger raised a $3.6M seed funding round led by Initialized Capital, with participation from prominent angel investors in the developer tools space. This followed a total of $6.6M raised over two seed rounds. The company is using the capital to expand its operations and product development. Recently, Digger launched Infrabase, an AI-powered DevOps agent that operates within pull requests to automate and simplify the infrastructure change lifecycle, from generating configuration code via prompts to flagging risks and ensuring compliance before deployment. 

Keywords: Infrastructure as Code, IaC orchestration, Terraform automation, OpenTofu, CI/CD integration, DevOps agent, GitOps, open source, pull request automation, drift detection, policy as code, OPA, cloud infrastructure management, Infrabase, TACOS, opentaco.dev, continuous deployment', '["Venture Capital","MLops","Subscription","SaaS","page builders","Angel","frameworks and libraries","development","content management system","online video platforms","it infrastructure","web frameworks","b2b","tag management","web servers","AI Model Deployment and Monitoring","content delivery network","programming languages","audio-video-graphics","web and application servers","miscellaneous","programming","caching","cloud infrastructure computing","javascript libraries","video players","Enterprise Software","analytics","issue trackers","operating systems & computing languages","cloud hosting","web analytics","platform as a service","uk is8 digital and technologies","javascript frameworks","consent management","Early stage","font scripts","blogs","ui frameworks","continuous integration"]', '["startup","rising_star","vc_backed","funded"]', 86.11, 110, 0, 0, 16363630, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[{"year":2021,"revenue":189394,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":null,"employees":7},{"year":2022,"revenue":625000,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":null,"employees":11},{"year":2023,"revenue":848485,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":null,"employees":18},{"year":2024,"revenue":848485,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":null,"employees":7},{"year":2025,"revenue":848485,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":16363630,"employees":16},{"year":2026,"revenue":848485,"ebitda":null,"profit":null,"rnd":null,"valuation":16363630,"employees":21}]', '2026-11-05T15:53:04.860Z', 'GBP', '2026-11-05T15:53:04.860Z');

 Financially, the platform launched with substantial backing, securing a total of $12 million from notable investors, including Breyer Capital, Atomico, and 21st Century Fox. The business model initially focused on leveraging the massive global automotive advertising market through native advertising and social commerce. It later evolved to a four-pronged approach encompassing content creation, distribution, data insights, and client engagement with major brands like Audi and Renault. The platform''s tech stack was built to extract real-time data analytics from user interactions, providing valuable insights to the automotive industry. 

 Despite rapid initial user growth, reaching 140 million monthly platform users by 2021, Drivetribe faced financial headwinds. The company reported significant losses in its early years and found sustained profitability challenging. In January 2022, facing industry-wide marketing budget cuts and the global chip shortage, the company announced the shutdown of its website and app. The brand, however, did not disappear. Richard Hammond acquired the Drivetribe brand, transitioning the business to focus on its successful social media channels, particularly YouTube. This strategic pivot was followed by the acquisition of video production agency Centric Creative in October 2023, repositioning Drivetribe as a full-service automotive content production and distribution house, offering end-to-end marketing solutions for automotive brands. 

