The core of the business is the "Gamebox," an interactive smart room designed for groups of two to six players. These rooms utilize a combination of projection mapping, touch-sensitive walls, 3D motion tracking, and surround sound to create immersive, shared adventures without the need for headsets. Players wear lightweight visors with tracking dots, allowing the system to follow their movements as they interact with the game projected on the 360-degree screens. The experience is designed to be collaborative, requiring teamwork to solve puzzles and complete challenges in 30-to-60-minute sessions. The target market is broad, encompassing families, couples on date nights, groups of friends, and corporate teams seeking team-building activities.

Revenue is generated through ticket sales for these gaming sessions, with prices around $30 per person for an hour. The company''s business model includes both company-owned venues and a franchise and concession model. This allows Immersive Gamebox to be integrated into existing entertainment centers, movie theaters, and malls. The firm has aggressively pursued global expansion, signing significant franchise deals to enter new markets. The company develops its game library in-house and through strategic IP partnerships with major entertainment studios like Netflix, Paramount, and Sony Pictures. This has led to the creation of popular games based on franchises such as "Squid Game," "Ghostbusters," "PAW Patrol," and "Angry Birds".

Keywords: social gaming, immersive entertainment, location-based entertainment, interactive gaming, franchise, projection mapping, motion tracking, group experiences, experiential retail, out-of-home entertainment', '["database","sales","Media","hris management","Venture Capital","web fonts","recruitment marketing","online form builders","marketing","marketing automation","customer relationship management","human resources","Angel","frameworks and libraries","content management system","other advertising software","online video platforms","marketing analytics","marketing performance measurement","it infrastructure","uk is8 creative industries","web platform extensions","tag management","widgets","search engines","content delivery network","programming languages","Breakout stage","audio-video-graphics","advertising","Pay Per Result","miscellaneous","programming","Ticketing","javascript libraries","wordpress plugins","intelligence and analytics","video players","analytics","Leisure","Gaming","web analytics","platform as a service","visualisation software","b2c","Social","Digital Entertainment","font scripts","social networks","blogs","applicant tracking systems","design","tools","captcha","web tools and plugins","web hosting"]', '["startup","vc_backed","funded"]', 79.72, 960, 0, 0, 49999980, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T15:53:18.240Z', 'GBP', '2026-11-05T15:53:18.240Z');

The founders'' drive was significantly influenced by their mentor, UW-Madison professor Gilbert Hemsley, a renowned lighting designer who inspired a culture of possibility. The company''s first major contracts came from Disney in the late 1970s and early 1980s to create lighting controls for their theme parks, which propelled ETC from a small startup to a key industry player. A pivotal moment in ETC''s history was the 1992 launch of the Source Four profile spotlight, a product that became an industry standard. Over the years, ETC has grown through strategic acquisitions, including LMI in 1990, the lighting control division of Arri GB in 1995, High End Systems in 2017, and GDS in 2019, which expanded its product portfolio into dimming, automated lighting, and backstage communication systems. In March 2024, ETC became a 100% employee-owned company through an Employee Stock Option Plan (ESOP), a move initiated years earlier by the late co-founder Fred Foster to ensure the company remained private and employee-focused.

ETC''s business model is centered on the design, manufacture, and sale of a wide array of professional tools for lighting and rigging. Its client base is diverse, serving small and large venues worldwide, including theaters, television studios, houses of worship, schools, hotels, casinos, and theme parks. The company generates revenue through the direct sale of its extensive product line, which includes lighting fixtures like the iconic Source Four, control consoles such as the Eos and Wholehog families, dimming and power control systems, networking infrastructure, and motorized rigging systems. ETC is also known for its comprehensive technical and customer service, offering 24/7 support and maintaining a global network of authorized service centers.

Keywords: lighting control, rigging technology, theatrical lighting, architectural lighting, entertainment lighting, stage lighting, LED fixtures, lighting consoles, dimmer systems, Source Four, Eos console, live event technology, AV solutions, performance lighting, automated lighting, stage equipment, lighting networking, power control, theatrical rigging, entertainment technology solutions', '["corporate","social media marketing software","database","online community software","corporate learning management systems","paas","Media","photo galleries","human resource management system","Venture Capital","system security services","e-commerce platforms","web fonts","recruitment marketing","editors","visual and user generated content marketing","Subscription","marketing automation","javascript graphics","human resources","Angel","frameworks and libraries","real estate property management","communication and collaboration","development","identity and access management","event management","content management system","other advertising software","job board software","online video platforms","software development","it infrastructure","web frameworks","source code management","shopping cart","ssl certificate","uk is8 creative industries","b2b","web platform extensions","tag management","corporate performance management","visualization software","team collaboration","widgets","web servers","search engines","software framework","hotel management","api management","Hard Tech","programming languages","Entertainment","audio-video-graphics","advertising","software","human resource","Manufacturing","website builders","site search","web & portal technology","web and application servers","miscellaneous","programming","caching","cloud infrastructure computing","business intelligence","e-commerce","office suites","dev tools","javascript libraries","wordpress plugins","intelligence and analytics","video players","Late stage","analytics","devops","network management (software)","securit