The firm''s business model is centered on providing game-based learning solutions to corporate clients for soft skills development. Revenue is generated through a subscription-based model, where clients gain access to Gamelearn''s entire catalog of games. The company serves a global market, with over 3,000 corporate clients across five continents, including notable names like LG, Fujitsu, and Hyundai. Gamelearn operates in the e-learning and gamification sector, offering a platform that can be integrated with a client''s existing Learning Management System (LMS) or used as a standalone solution.

Gamelearn''s core offering is a cloud-based platform that combines quality content, advanced simulators, and gamification elements like challenges and rankings to enhance employee engagement. The platform includes an extensive catalog of ready-made "Serious Games" focused on in-demand professional skills such as time management, negotiation, leadership, and team management. For instance, "Triskelion" is an adventure game designed to teach personal productivity, while "Pacific" focuses on managing high-performance teams. A key differentiator is the "Editor," an authoring tool launched in 2020 that enables clients to create their own customized training video games from scratch without needing programming skills. This tool provides a library of 3D assets and templates, allowing companies to convert their specific content into interactive, multi-language training simulations accessible on any device. The platform is designed to yield tangible results, with clients reporting high course completion rates, often above 90%, and direct applicability of learned skills.

