The firm targets medium and large companies, helping them measure, reduce, and certify their carbon emissions to comply with evolving environmental regulations and access new financing and tenders. Manglai''s business model is centered on its software platform, which simplifies the carbon footprint management process. The platform provides real-time calculation of carbon emissions across scopes 1, 2, and 3, and uses artificial intelligence for the automatic analysis and classification of invoices, which streamlines data management. It also offers personalized recommendations and interactive dashboards for impact analysis. This service is designed to be faster and more efficient than traditional environmental consultancies or other digital solutions. 

Manglai''s client base includes investment funds with ESG criteria, environmental consultancies, and corporations seeking to meet sustainability goals. The company has secured $539K in a seed funding round on February 19, 2024, led by All Iron. A case study involving Carter Cranes highlights the platform''s ability to provide a complete view of emissions, simplify sustainability reporting, and strengthen a client''s brand image. 

Keywords: carbon footprint management, sustainability software, AI for sustainability, emissions reduction, carbon accounting, ESG reporting, corporate sustainability, CleanTech, environmental regulation compliance, carbon certification, scope 1 2 3 emissions, decarbonization platform, environmental data analysis, sustainability solutions, net-zero emissions, carbon measurement, green technology, sustainable business, climate tech, ESG data management, All Iron, Andres Cester, Jaume Fontal', '["data management","database","sales","Venture Capital","mobile application development platforms","Core AI","Subscription","SaaS","sales enablement","performance","b2b","Artificial Intelligence","Energy","programming languages","AI Applications","javascript libraries","intelligence and analytics","analytics","web analytics","Energy Efficiency","Early stage","certificates","cookie compliance"]', '["startup","rising_star","vc_backed","funded"]', 93.51, 64000, 1, 2, 4999998, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-11-05T15:55:36.049Z', 'GBP', '2026-11-05T15:55:36.049Z');

 The company''s core offering is an all-in-one design and prototyping tool that enables users to transform static design files and even paper sketches into interactive, shareable prototypes without writing any code. It serves a diverse clientele, ranging from individual freelancers and students to large enterprise teams at companies like Stripe, Monzo, and BuzzFeed. Marvel''s business model is based on a freemium structure; a free version is available for single users with limited projects, while paid subscription tiers (Pro, Team, and Enterprise) unlock additional features, unlimited projects, and remove branding. Revenue is generated through these monthly or annual subscriptions. 

 Marvel''s platform is equipped with a suite of features including wireframing, design, prototyping, user testing, and collaboration tools. Users can upload designs from Sketch or Photoshop, or design directly within Marvel using its library of assets and templates. The platform facilitates the creation of realistic prototypes with transitions and gestures for various devices, including iPhones, iPads, and desktops. A key feature is the "Handoff" tool, which generates CSS, Swift, and Android XML code from designs, streamlining the transition from design to development. The platform also emphasizes collaboration, allowing team members to comment directly on prototypes and providing a centralized space for feedback. To enhance its workflow, Marvel integrates with numerous popular tools such as Jira, Slack, Dropbox, and Microsoft Teams. The company has also made strategic acquisitions, including the prototyping tool POP in 2016 and design tool Plexi in 2015, to expand its user base and capabilities. 

