Typeform serves a diverse range of clients across various industries, including automotive, computer software, consumer goods, education, entertainment, financial services, food and beverage, government administration, healthcare, IT, marketing, nonprofits, professional services, public relations, real estate, sports, transportation, and travel hospitality. The company also caters to different roles within these organizations, such as marketing, human resources, product management, customer success, and education.

The business model of Typeform is subscription-based. Clients pay a recurring fee to access and use Typeform''s suite of tools. This model ensures a steady stream of revenue and allows the company to continuously improve and expand its offerings. Additionally, Typeform partners with various agencies to help clients integrate their tools into existing workflows, ensuring they get the most out of the platform.

Typeform makes money primarily through these subscription fees. They offer different pricing tiers based on the features and level of service required by the client. This allows businesses of all sizes, from small startups to large enterprises, to find a plan that fits their needs and budget.

