The business model hinges on delivering a curated selection of products to customers within minutes, 24/7. Revenue is generated through the sale and delivery of these goods, which include everyday essentials, groceries, snacks, over-the-counter medicines, and a distinct selection of luxury and premium items from brands like Apple, LVMH, and Daylesford Organic. A flat delivery fee is applied to orders, which is waived for purchases exceeding a certain value, reportedly £30. This model targets a ''cash-rich, time-poor'' customer segment that values speed and product quality. By focusing on higher-margin products and impulse buys, the company reportedly achieves a strong average order value. 

Operationally, Zapp utilizes its own micro-fulfilment centers, known as ''Zappstores''. These are strategically located, delivery-only dark stores stocked with thousands of items, enabling the company to maintain control over its inventory and ensure rapid picking and packing. The company supports this with a proprietary supply chain, including a large distribution center in London to restock its Zappstores, and employs its riders directly, using a fleet of electric vehicles. Since its inception, Zapp has raised a total of $300 million over several funding rounds, with a significant $200 million Series B round in early 2022. The funding was co-led by investors such as Lightspeed Venture Partners, 468 Capital, and BroadLight Capital, with notable participation from Atomico and Sir Lewis Hamilton. 

Keywords: quick commerce, on-demand delivery, convenience retail, urban logistics, dark store, 24/7 delivery, premium goods, grocery delivery, last-mile delivery, e-commerce, fulfillment centers, London startup, Navid Hadzaad, Joe Falter, direct-to-consumer, mobile commerce, essential goods, food delivery, Zappstores, venture capital, premium convenience', '["Rumoured $1B","human resource management system","Venture Capital","web fonts","recruitment marketing","Food Logistics & Delivery","marketing automation","human resources","Angel","10 Min Delivery","email","programming languages","Commission","Groceries","business intelligence","governance-risk and compliance","intelligence and analytics","customer experience management","Late stage","analytics","operating systems & computing languages","visualisation software","b2c","applicant tracking systems","design","tools","email marketing","Food","operating systems","web tools and plugins"]', '["startup","colt","vc_backed","funded"]', 73.47, 25000, 0, 0, 909090545, -75078000, 2021, -23038261, 2023, '[{"year":2021,"revenue":11508000,"ebitda":-75078000,"profit":-76154000,"rnd":null,"valuation":454545273,"employees":null},{"year":2022,"revenue":28931000,"ebitda":-75078000,"profit":-91944000,"rnd":null,"valuation":909090545,"employees":357},{"year":2023,"revenue":39463234,"ebitda":-75078000,"profit":-23038261,"rnd":null,"valuation":909090545,"employees":347},{"year":2024,"revenue":39463234,"ebitda":-75078000,"profit":-23038261,"rnd":null,"valuation":909090545,"employees":377},{"year":2025,"revenue":39463234,"ebitda":-75078000,"profit":-23038261,"rnd":null,"valuation":909090545,"employees":374},{"year":2026,"revenue":39463234,"ebitda":-75078000,"profit":-23038261,"rnd":null,"valuation":909090545,"employees":381}]', '2026-11-05T15:54:51.538Z', 'GBP', '2026-11-05T15:54:51.538Z');

The company''s initial focus was on providing pay-as-you-go insurance for delivery drivers and riders, a segment often underserved by conventional insurers. Zego''s business model revolves around leveraging data and telematics to offer usage-based and behavior-based insurance policies. This approach allows for more accurate risk assessment, theoretically enabling fairer pricing for safer drivers. Revenue is generated through the sale of these insurance policies, which range from flexible per-minute or per-hour coverage to fixed-term contracts for fleets. 

Zego serves a diverse client base, from individual self-employed drivers and riders in the gig economy to large commercial fleets. The company has formed partnerships with prominent platforms like Uber, Deliveroo, and Bolt, integrating its insurance products to provide seamless coverage for their drivers. Operating in the insurtech market, Zego has expanded its services across several European countries, including the UK, Ireland, France, and the Netherlands. 

A significant milestone in Zego''s history was achieving a valuation of $1.1 billion in 2021, making it the first UK insurtech to attain unicorn status. This was accomplished following a Series C funding round that raised $150 million, led by DST Global. In 2023, the company secured a further $11.5 million in funding to support its growth and path to profitability. The core product is a data-led insurance platform that utilizes telematics data from driver apps or integrated hardware to analyze driving behaviors such as speed, acceleration, and braking, creating a driver score that influences policy pricing. 

