The founding team includes CEO Roger Antunez, who was the General Manager of BCN3D, and co-founder Marta Mico, formerly the VLM Head of Business Development at BCN3D. Eric Pallares, the former CTO of BCN3D, joined as a Distinguished Technologist. Supernova''s mission is to produce end-use parts at scale using its proprietary VLM technology, which processes high-viscosity resins to create parts with properties comparable to injection-molded plastics. 

Supernova''s core offering is the Pulse Production Platform, which is built on its patented VLM technology. This platform includes the Pulse One printer, a post-processing cell, and a control center, creating an automated, end-to-end production system. The system uses proprietary photopolymer materials called Viscogels, which include rigid composites, ductile materials, rubbers, and silicones. This closed ecosystem approach, from materials to post-processing, is designed to guarantee the quality and traceability of the final components. The technology allows for processing resins up to 100 times more viscous than traditional systems, enabling the production of parts with superior mechanical properties. Key features include dual-material printing for 2K parts, which eliminates the need for overmolding tooling, and the use of a secondary support material for a better surface finish. 

The company serves industrial customers across various sectors, including transportation, consumer goods, and manufacturing services. It has established partnerships with prominent companies like Puma, Saint Gobain, Hutchinson, and Prodrive through a Technology Adoption Program that attracted over 500 companies. Supernova also has a Defense & Space division focused on developing an additive manufacturing ecosystem for energetic materials, leveraging its technology to process materials with high solid loads for national security and space applications. This division has been awarded a $2 million subcontract to 3D print military-grade energetic materials. 

